{"answers":[{"question_id":"Q016_pdb_1acc_domain_split","answer":"In SCOP 1.75, PDB 1acc (anthrax protective antigen, Bacillus anthracis) chain A was a single whole-chain domain, d1acca_ (sunid 43828, description '1acc A:'). It was classified as f.11.1.1: class f (membrane and cell surface proteins and peptides), fold/superfamily/family 'Anthrax protective antigen' (f.11, f.11.1, f.11.1.1). The live API shows the same single domain in releases 2.01, 2.02 and 2.03. From SCOPe 2.04 onward (checked in 2.04–2.08) the chain is split into two domains. d1acca1 (sunid 238521) covers A:226-735 and is f.60.1.1, the family 'Anthrax protective antigen C-terminal-like', in fold/superfamily f.60 / f.60.1. d1acca2 (sunid 238522) covers A:14-225 and is b.179.1.1, the family 'PA14', in superfamily b.179.1 'PA14-like' and fold b.179 'Anthrax protective antigen N-terminal-like'. This is a re-parsing and reclassification, not a simple relabel. The old d1acca_ sunid is replaced by two new domain sunids with new sids. The two pieces fall in different classes: one in the membrane/cell-surface class f and one in the all-beta class b. The single-domain label hid that.

What it reveals: a SCOP domain is an evolutionary and structural module, not necessarily a whole chain or a gene product. One polypeptide can hold several modules with different ancestry, fold and evolutionary relatives. Whole-chain assignments in early releases, and the single class 'f' label, can be wrong for part of the chain. The family/superfamily/fold labels apply to the module. Sequence residue ranges and domain boundaries, not the PDB chain, define the homology unit. The split also shows that SCOPe is revised as more structures and evidence arrive. The domain boundaries and the classifications are expert-curated hypotheses, and releases can differ. The N-terminal PA14 module (b.179.1.1) is a separate module from the C-terminal f.60 module. Both reside in the same 735-residue chain but they are not shown to be homologous to each other. Family and superfamily placement of each piece does not tell you that the other piece is related.

Implications for homology searches: (1) Do not transfer a classification or function from a hit onto a whole query chain. Search and annotate per domain, using the domain residue ranges (A:14-225 and A:226-735), and map hits to the matching region only. (2) Use the current release (2.08 here, with periodic updates through 2026-08-16) and record the release used. Do not mix SCOP 1.75 labels with SCOPe 2.04+ labels, and do not treat f.11.1.1 as the current assignment. When working with old data, map the old sunid or sid to the new domains via the PDB code and residue ranges. (3) Family-level placement supports close homology and likely function transfer only within that domain family. Superfamily placement indicates a probable common ancestor but weaker functional transfer. Fold similarity alone does not prove homology. (4) A hit covering only one part of the chain is not evidence that the whole chain is homologous, so check coverage per domain. For multidomain queries, use domain-aware tools such as profile or HMM searches against SCOPe domains, and check alignment coverage and E-values. For related proteins, a different domain architecture may mean different function. Pull any domain-level inference from the correct domain only, and confirm with sequence, structure and functional evidence (for example, experimental data) before assuming shared function. (5) For benchmarks or training sets, split or cluster by domain and by current classification, not by chain or PDB code. Otherwise, domains from the same family or superfamily can leak across train/test sets, and old whole-chain labels can introduce errors.","evidence_used":["/api/v1/releases","/api/v1/search?q=1acc","/api/v1/releases/2.08/pdb/1acc/domains","/api/v1/releases/1.75/pdb/1acc/domains","/api/v1/releases/2.01/pdb/1acc/domains","/api/v1/releases/2.02/pdb/1acc/domains","/api/v1/releases/2.03/pdb/1acc/domains","/api/v1/releases/2.04/pdb/1acc/domains","/api/v1/releases/2.05/pdb/1acc/domains","/api/v1/releases/